\chapter{The Companion LaTeX Package}
\label{chap:sty}

Everything so far lives on the Python side: an author calls a helper from
PythonTeX and prints the LaTeX it returns.  Two jobs cannot be done from
there, because they need information that only TeX has.
\begin{description}
\item[Following the [[import]] package] A deck bound into a book with
  [[\subimport{../modules/deck/}{contents.tex}]] should write its files
  into the deck's directory (\cref{sec:base-directory}).  Only TeX knows
  which directory is being imported at a given point: the [[import]]
  package keeps it in [[\import@path]], which Python never sees.
\item[Reading generated snippets] A Beamer frame that shows a question
  should [[\input]] a file written by [[write_snippet]] rather than run
  PythonTeX inside the frame, and it must survive the first compile,
  before that file exists.
\end{description}
The package [[mentipy.sty]] covers both.  It is deliberately thin: it
decides \emph{when} and \emph{with what value} to call the Python side,
and all path logic stays in [[resolve_path]], so the two sides cannot
disagree about where a file goes.

A document loads it after PythonTeX, and a book that binds decks turns on
the automatic base directory once in its preamble:

\begin{minted}{latex}
\usepackage{import}
\usepackage{pythontex}
\usepackage{mentipy}
\mentipybasedir{auto}
\end{minted}

The package is tangled from this file into the Python package, and
[[mentipy sty]] prints it or installs it into the user's TeX tree, since
TeX cannot find a file inside a Python wheel.

<<[[mentipy.sty]]>>=
\NeedsTeXFormat{LaTeX2e}[2020/10/01]
\ProvidesPackage{mentipy}[2026/09/25 Companion package for Mentipy]
<<package dependencies>>
<<base directory>>
<<question snippets>>
\endinput
@

\section{Loading the Python side}

The package talks to Python through PythonTeX, the same way the didactic
package does: TeX macros emit [[\pyc{...}]] code that runs, in document
order, in PythonTeX's default session.  We therefore require PythonTeX
without options; a document that needs options loads PythonTeX itself
first, and the [[\RequirePackage]] is then a no-op.

Importing Mentipy right away is not just a convenience.  A relative base
directory is anchored at the working directory in force when Mentipy is
first imported (\cref{sec:base-directory}), and code emitted while the
preamble loads runs before any chapter's code.  The anchor is therefore
the directory the session started in, which is also where the didactic
package anchors its [[\runbasedir]], so the two packages agree on what a
relative path means.

<<package dependencies>>=
\RequirePackage{pythontex}
\pyc{import mentipy}
@

\section{The base directory}

The user-facing macro takes a path, the word [[auto]], or nothing.
\begin{description}
\item[A path] resolves Mentipy's files against that directory; a relative
  one is anchored at the session start.
\item[ [[auto]] ] follows the [[import]] package: inside
  [[\subimport{../deck/}{...}]] it means [[../deck/]], and outside any
  import it means no base directory at all.  A deck therefore compiles
  unchanged standalone and inside the book.
\item[Empty] switches the base directory off.
\end{description}

The setting is local, like any TeX definition: set inside a group or an
environment, it ends with that group.  This matches the didactic
package's [[\runbasedir]], so a chapter macro can set both in the same
place and let the chapter's group undo both.

<<base directory>>=
\newcommand{\mentipybasedir}[1]{%
  \def\mentipy@basedir{#1}%
  \mentipy@sync
}
\def\mentipy@basedir{}
\def\mentipy@auto{auto}
@

\subsection{When Python learns the value}

Python cannot follow TeX's groups: a Python variable set from inside a
group stays set after the group ends.  So we cannot simply forward each
[[\mentipybasedir]] to Python and be done.  Instead the package
\emph{synchronises} Python with the value TeX has in force at every
point where Python code is about to run: at the start of every PythonTeX
code environment and inline code command, and at [[\mentipybasedir]]
itself.  The rule an author can rely on is therefore: \emph{a Mentipy
call sees the base directory in force where its PythonTeX code begins.}

A synchronisation emits code only when the value actually changes.  This
is what keeps the package harmless in Beamer, which reads a frame's body
once per overlay: re-reading the frame repeats the synchronisation but
finds nothing to send, so the extra reads add no PythonTeX commands and
do not shift PythonTeX's numbering.  The last value sent is kept
globally, because it describes Python's state, which groups do not
restore.

<<base directory>>=
\gdef\mentipy@synced{}
\newif\ifmentipy@syncing
\newcommand{\mentipy@sync}{%
  \ifmentipy@syncing\else
    <<compute the effective base directory>>
    \ifx\mentipy@value\mentipy@synced\else
      \global\let\mentipy@synced\mentipy@value
      <<send the value to Python>>
    \fi
  \fi
}
@

The effective value expands [[auto]] to the current [[\import@path]].
We expand it now, with [[\edef]], because the import path changes as
soon as the import ends; outside any import, or when the [[import]]
package is not loaded at all, [[auto]] means no base directory.

<<compute the effective base directory>>=
\ifx\mentipy@basedir\mentipy@auto
  \ifdefined\import@path
    \edef\mentipy@value{\import@path}%
  \else
    \def\mentipy@value{}%
  \fi
\else
  \let\mentipy@value\mentipy@basedir
\fi
@

Sending the value is itself a [[\pyc]], so it triggers the hook on
[[\pyc]] below; the [[\ifmentipy@syncing]] guard stops that recursion.
The path travels as a raw Python string, so backslashes need no
escaping, and the empty string is what [[set_base_dir]] reads as
\enquote{off}.

<<send the value to Python>>=
\mentipy@syncingtrue
\expandafter\mentipy@send\expandafter{\mentipy@value}%
\mentipy@syncingfalse
@

<<base directory>>=
\newcommand{\mentipy@send}[1]{%
  \pyc{mentipy.set_base_dir(r'''#1''')}%
}
@

\subsection{The synchronisation points}

PythonTeX defines its code environments with [[\newenvironment]], so
LaTeX's generic environment hooks run before each of them.  The inline
commands take generic command hooks.  We hook the Python family's
commands that execute code and leave out the verbatim ones, which only
typeset.  All code goes to PythonTeX's default session, so a deck that
runs Mentipy in a named session gets no synchronisation; the base
directory then has to be set in that session with [[set_base_dir]].

<<base directory>>=
\AddToHook{env/pycode/before}{\mentipy@sync}
\AddToHook{env/pyblock/before}{\mentipy@sync}
\AddToHook{env/pysub/before}{\mentipy@sync}
\AddToHook{env/pyconsole/before}{\mentipy@sync}
\AddToHook{cmd/py/before}{\mentipy@sync}
\AddToHook{cmd/pyc/before}{\mentipy@sync}
\AddToHook{cmd/pys/before}{\mentipy@sync}
\AddToHook{cmd/pyb/before}{\mentipy@sync}
@

The [[auto]] mode relies on one assumption: that PythonTeX's working
directory is the document's directory, so that a path relative to the
document, like [[\import@path]], is also relative to the session start.
That is PythonTeX's default.  A document that moves the working
directory with [[\setpythontexworkingdir]] should give
[[\mentipybasedir]] explicit paths instead.

\section{Question snippets}

The frame-side half of [[write_snippet]] reads the snippet if it exists.
On the very first compile it does not, because PythonTeX runs after
LaTeX, so a missing snippet is a warning rather than an error: the next
compile after PythonTeX has run picks it up.  The lookup goes through
[[\IfFileExists]] and [[\input]], which both search [[\input@path]], so
inside a [[\subimport]] the snippet is found in the deck's directory
just like the QR sidecars it inputs in turn.

<<question snippets>>=
\newcommand{\mentipyinput}[1]{%
  \IfFileExists{\mentipy@snippetdir/#1.tex}%
    {\input{\mentipy@snippetdir/#1.tex}}%
    {\PackageWarning{mentipy}{Snippet `#1' not written yet;
      rerun LaTeX after PythonTeX}}%
}
@

[[write_snippet]] writes into the configured QR directory unless told
otherwise, and TeX cannot read Mentipy's configuration file.  The default
here is therefore the built-in default, [[mentipy-obj]]; a document
whose configuration or [[directory]] argument says otherwise states the
same directory with [[\mentipysnippetdir]].

<<question snippets>>=
\newcommand{\mentipysnippetdir}[1]{\def\mentipy@snippetdir{#1}}
\mentipysnippetdir{mentipy-obj}
@

\section{Testing the package}

The macros only mean something once LaTeX, PythonTeX and the Python
side run together, so the test compiles a real book that binds a deck.
It needs [[pdflatex]] and [[pythontex]] and is skipped where they are
missing, which keeps the unit suite runnable without a TeX installation.

<<test [[sty.py]]>>=
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
from importlib import resources

import pytest

<<test functions>>
@

<<test functions>>=
needs_tex = pytest.mark.skipif(
    not (shutil.which("pdflatex") and shutil.which("pythontex")),
    reason="needs pdflatex and pythontex",
)


def compile_with_pythontex(directory, name):
    def run(*command):
        return subprocess.run(
            command, cwd=directory, capture_output=True, text=True
        )

    latex = ("pdflatex", "-interaction=nonstopmode", "-halt-on-error", name)
    run(*latex)
    python = run(
        "pythontex", "--interpreter", f"python:{sys.executable}", name
    )
    return python, run(*latex)
@

The fixture is the situation from the base-directory section: the book
[[\subimport]]s a deck, the deck builds its store and writes a snippet
with paths relative to itself, and the frame-side [[\mentipyinput]]
reads the snippet back, which in turn inputs the QR sidecar from the
deck.  After the import, the book records which base
directory Python sees, to check that leaving the import switched it off
again.

<<test functions>>=
BOOK = r"""\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{import}
\usepackage{mentipy}
\mentipybasedir{auto}
\begin{document}
\subimport{../deck/}{contents.tex}
\begin{pycode}
from mentipy.store import get_base_dir
open("after-import.txt", "w").write(repr(get_base_dir()))
\end{pycode}
\end{document}
"""

DECK = r"""\begin{pycode}
from mentipy.latex import mc, write_snippet
from mentipy.store import Store
write_snippet("q1", mc(
    "Ready?", ["Yes", "No"],
    store=Store("mentipy.json"),
    base_url="http://example.test:8080",
    qr_dir="mentipy-obj",
    layout="article+qr",
))
\end{pycode}
\mentipyinput{q1}
"""


@needs_tex
def test_auto_base_dir_keeps_a_bound_decks_files_in_the_deck(
    tmp_path, monkeypatch
):
    monkeypatch.setenv("MENTIPY_CONFIG", str(tmp_path / "config.json"))
    book, deck = tmp_path / "book", tmp_path / "deck"
    book.mkdir()
    deck.mkdir()
    sty = resources.files("mentipy").joinpath("mentipy.sty")
    (book / "mentipy.sty").write_text(sty.read_text())
    (book / "book.tex").write_text(BOOK)
    (deck / "contents.tex").write_text(DECK)

    python, latex = compile_with_pythontex(book, "book.tex")

    assert "- 0 error(s)" in python.stdout, python.stdout[-2000:]
    assert latex.returncode == 0, latex.stdout[-2000:]
    assert "Package mentipy Warning" not in latex.stdout
    assert (deck / "mentipy.json").exists()
    assert (deck / "mentipy-obj" / "q1.tex").exists()
    assert list((deck / "mentipy-obj").glob("Q-*.pdf_tex"))
    assert not (book / "mentipy.json").exists()
    assert not (book / "mentipy-obj").exists()
    assert (book / "after-import.txt").read_text() == "None"
@
